## What works

Status on the test unit (one 512 GB unit, model 25091RP04C) with `$kernel_release`; see the
[support matrix]($repo_url/blob/$integration_commit/README.md#support-matrix) for the details and limits.

- ✅ Working: CPU frequency scaling, boot from slot A (marked successful after each boot), UFS storage,
  GNOME on Wayland at 200 % scaling, Adreno 830 rendering with the bundled Mesa 26.2.0, battery gauges
  and thermal zones, SSH over USB in developer mode.
- 🟡 Partial: display (firmware framebuffer, 3200×2136 at 120 Hz, fixed brightness, no screen-off),
  touchscreen (single finger, needs your calibration), magnetic keyboard (keys), Wi-Fi (firmware and scan,
  needs your MAC address), Bluetooth (discovery, needs your address), switching from Ubuntu to Android,
  charger input reporting (read-only), power key.
- ❌ Unsupported: charging (the battery discharges under Linux, also on USB), speakers, cameras, sensors,
  video decoding, USB host and external display, suspend, writing the real-time clock (the clock is wrong
  after every boot), fingerprint, stylus. 🧪 Unverified: microphone.

## Per-unit data you must add

The image and the boot set contain no data of any unit: no account, serial number, Bluetooth or Wi-Fi
address, touch calibration, boot profile, partition map or signing key of yours. Until you add them:

| File in `/etc/piano/` (root, mode 0600) | Without it |
|---|---|
| `thp-calibration.json` | the touchscreen does not start (use the keyboard) |
| `wlan-address` | Wi-Fi cannot be brought up: this unit's WCN7860 reports an all-zero MAC address; ath12k reads the address from the boot DTB, which `piano-boot-update` writes from this file. A kernel-side fallback to a random address is planned |
| `bluetooth-address` | the Bluetooth controller stays unconfigured |
| `boot-profile.json`, `boot-signing.pem` | `piano-boot-update` cannot rebuild the boot set, so the addresses above are not applied and kernel updates leave the set unchanged |
| `boot-partitions.json` | `piano-boot-switch` (switching from Ubuntu, the app-grid button) refuses |

After adding them run `sudo piano-boot-update $kernel_release`; it rebuilds the set with your addresses and
key and rewrites slot A. Your stock Android set goes into the store with
`sudo piano-boot-switch import-android --from <directory>`.

The prebuilt boot set fits stock `$stock_build` only. With another stock build, rebuild the set from
your own boot profile before writing slot A.

## Known issues

- No charging under Linux: keep at least 40 % and charge in Android.
- Release boots have no debug console. Developer mode (SSH over USB) is installed but off;
  enable it with `sudo piano-devmode on` before relying on remote access.
- With no picture or no response, hold Power + Volume Down for Fastboot and switch with `piano-switch.py`.
- The Android-side KernelSU module (`piano-switch-ksu`) is not yet verified on the device.
- Not verified on the device as a whole: this image was assembled offline from the packages and
  procedure that run on the test unit. It was checked offline and booted in QEMU (no piano hardware),
  where the initrd mounted the root and `/home` by partition name; Ubuntu's first-boot snap seeding
  did not finish within the 20-minute emulation budget, so `multi-user.target` was not observed.

The firmware package carries unmodified stock files of `$stock_build`; there is no redistribution
licence for them, and they are removed on request of the rights holder. Integration code is MIT,
the kernel GPL-2.0.
